<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chastana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chastana was a Saka ruler of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the early 2nd century CE who founded the Kardamaka line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>. Known from coins and inscriptions, he may first have governed as a Kushan subordinate. The geographer Ptolemy mentions a ruler called Tiastanes at Ozene, generally identified with him. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i">Rudradaman I</a> made the dynasty a major power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chastana.jpg" alt="Chastana" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Chastana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chashtana's name is attested in Greek as Siastansas, the form written in the corrupted Greek of his coins, and as Tiastanēs in Ptolemy, in Brahmi as Caṣṭana and in Kharosthi as Chaṭhaṇa, forms derived from a Saka name meaning master. The bards called him Tisman, a spelling closer to the Greek. His coins name his father as Ysamotika.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ptolemy's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Geographia</a>, which classes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a>, describes Chashtana's territory as reaching from Patalene in the west to his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, Ozene, in the east, and beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Barygaza</a> in the south. According to Ptolemy he ruled Ujjain directly, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paithan">Paithan</a>, Pratisthana, was held by Siristolemaios, identified with Sri Pulumayi, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a>; his rule thus fell in the reign of the Satavahana king Vasisthiputra Sri Pulamavi. A statue found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> together with statues of the Kushan kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> and Vima Taktu, bearing the name Shastana, is often attributed to him.</p><p>His coins carry his portrait with a corrupted Greek legend, a rendering of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> for King and Satrap Chashtana. The reverse bears a Brahmi legend, Of the Rajah, the Great Satrap, son of Ysamotika, Chashtana, around a symbol of three hills and a river with the sun and two moons, sometimes followed by his name in Kharosthi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Chashtana founded the Kardamaka, or Bhadramukha, line, one of the two major Saka satrap dynasties of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The other, the short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshaharata-dynasty">Kshaharatas</a>, included Bhumaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahapana">Nahapana</a>. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i">Rudradaman I</a> became the great conqueror among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>. Ptolemy still knew Chashtana as Tiastanēs, ruling a large area of western India into the 2nd century CE, especially the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chashtana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A second line, the Kardamakas, was founded by <b>Chastana</b> in Ujjain and became the main dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a></li></ul></section>
